WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:12.818
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.365 --> 00:00:23.240
MATT DUFTLER: Thank you
everybody for coming.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.560
My name is Matt Duftler.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.940
I'm a software engineer
for Google out of New York.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.140
I'm here today with Andy
Glover, an engineering

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.480
manager for Netflix.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.270
And we're here to talk
to you about Spinnaker.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.260
Spinnaker is a recently
released open source

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.890
continuous delivery platform.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:38.750
It supports multiple
cloud platforms

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.100
out the box, including
comprehensive support

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.146
for Google Compute Engine.

00:00:44.146 --> 00:00:46.270
So before we dive into the
particulars of Spinnaker

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.020
itself, let's talk a
bit about the problem

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.135
we're trying to solve.

00:00:49.135 --> 00:00:52.200
And that is managing the
complexity of cloud deployments

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.030
and cloud environments.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.096
Make no mistake about it, cloud
deployments are complicated.

00:00:57.096 --> 00:00:58.470
We talk to customers
quite often,

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.460
and one thing we
hear constantly is

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.590
that they'd like to
increase developer velocity.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.315
What this amounts to is dealing
with the volume of change.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.420
Terms of decreasing-- excuse me.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.592
In terms of increasing
developer velocity,

00:01:14.592 --> 00:01:16.300
the key is to reduce
the friction of time

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.424
between developers making
changes and those changes

00:01:18.424 --> 00:01:20.347
landing in production.

00:01:20.347 --> 00:01:22.680
There are many developers
changing systems all the time,

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.596
pushing these changes.

00:01:23.596 --> 00:01:25.350
And this is only
going up over time.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.890
As organizations add developers,
the volume of change increases.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.960
And we try to reduce
friction in time

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.240
as these changes are
pushed to various stages

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.486
in the production.

00:01:35.486 --> 00:01:37.110
In addition, you have
multiple services

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.670
being deployed independently.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.520
Organizations are moving
to a micro services model.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.370
And as these services
are deployed,

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:45.124
you have potentially
an exponential number

00:01:45.124 --> 00:01:46.790
of changes being
pushed into production,

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:49.900
an exponentially increasing
number of changes.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.411
In an ideal world,
changes to these services,

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:54.160
as they're rolled out
or isolated from one

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.760
another through a
robust set of APIs--

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.540
Still, this
increases complexity.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.289
The volume of change
is always increasing.

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:03.980
There are always more
services coming online.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.050
Also keep in mind, it's
not a static world.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:07.502
Things are always changing.

00:02:07.502 --> 00:02:09.710
You're typically deploying
to cloud environments that

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:11.264
are being dynamically scaled.

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:12.680
And the days when
you can turn off

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.250
your service to do upgrades
or make changes are long gone.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.125
So you need to be able
to gracefully roll out

00:02:17.125 --> 00:02:19.420
your changes while
you're continuing

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.490
to service your users while
your cloud footprint may

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:24.649
be scaling in response to load.

00:02:24.649 --> 00:02:26.690
And lastly you need to be
able to revert changes.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.856
So if something goes wrong
as you do these rollouts,

00:02:28.856 --> 00:02:31.810
you need to be able to
gracefully and quickly rollback

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.190
before you do damage
to your business.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.830
So how do we handle
this complexity?

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:39.200
It's a combination of
principles, practices,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.180
and tools.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.210
The key principle is
immutable infrastructure.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.710
Andy's going to
come up in a minutes

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.543
and he's going to talk
about how they employ

00:02:45.543 --> 00:02:49.530
this principle at Netflix
using this tool Spinnaker,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.840
which we're going to go into a
lot of detail and show demos.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.880
But first, let's talk about
immutable infrastructure.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.870
So the way things work, you
typically have a machine.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:01.850
It has CPU resources,
memory, disk.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.345
Some of this is virtual,
some potentially real.

00:03:04.345 --> 00:03:05.470
We have that machine layer.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.870
On top of that, you have
an operating system layer.

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:09.820
You have whatever
libraries and applications

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.450
your application depends,
on typically something

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.370
like an app or a web server.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.740
And then you have your
actual application.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.440
And each of these
different layers

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:23.400
can be deployed onto these
machines using different tools.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.632
Before cloud computing
really went mainstream,

00:03:25.632 --> 00:03:27.590
you would typically use
something like a config

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:28.495
management system.

00:03:28.495 --> 00:03:30.370
You would use that to
manage your web and app

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:33.210
server, and then maybe
another specialized tool

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:34.640
to deploy your application.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.220
When you would roll
out changes, you

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:37.950
would connect to
those running machines

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.260
and redeploy the
application with the changes

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.190
onto that running system.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.507
With the advent of cloud
computing and companies

00:03:45.507 --> 00:03:47.340
moving to an immutable
infrastructure model,

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:48.410
you would never do this.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.810
The idea is that in
the old days when

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.000
it was expensive and slow
to build new machines,

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.760
you'd try to avoid building
new machines from scratch.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.700
Now that it's fast and cheap
to rollout new machines,

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.800
you're provisioning new machines
all the time in the cloud.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.640
So tearing down a machine,
provisioning a new machine,

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.011
installing all of your software,
this is fast and cheap and easy

00:04:08.011 --> 00:04:08.510
now.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:10.676
So when you roll out a new
version of your software,

00:04:10.676 --> 00:04:13.340
instead of modifying things
in place, which is risky,

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.890
you take the
approach where I have

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.589
my new version of software.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:19.880
Let me bake a static machine
image with that software.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.569
Let me move that image
into a test cluster.

00:04:22.569 --> 00:04:24.680
Let me run my tests against it.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:28.470
Once I'm happy, I stop traffic
from reaching the old version.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.770
Traffic's only reaching
the new version.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.100
And later on, you can go
off and gracefully destroy

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.370
that old infrastructure.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:38.004
Anything goes wrong
along the way,

00:04:38.004 --> 00:04:39.170
you can easily roll it back.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:43.690
Just direct traffic back
to that old cluster.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:47.500
So at Google, we have tens
of thousands of developers.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.740
It's 30,000 plus developers
changing things all the time.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.020
We have a principle where
any developer can change

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.190
any part of the code base.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:56.760
We have something like a Linux
kernel's worth of changes

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.710
landing in production weekly.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:00.850
So how do we manage
all this change?

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.660
Well, we bake new images.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:03.830
We manage traffic.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.810
We don't modify
systems in place.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.300
Other companies following these
same principles, companies

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.630
like Netflix, have been
applying the concepts

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.040
of immutable infrastructure.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.230
It's been working
very well for them.

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:16.670
The majority of
their deployments

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:18.845
are done employing
immutable infrastructure.

00:05:18.845 --> 00:05:20.970
And the tool they use
primarily to make this happen

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:21.830
is Spinnaker.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:23.360
And this is their flagship tool.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:26.700
It's a centerpiece of
their cloud platform.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.510
And Andy's going to
come up and tell us

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:29.551
quite a bit about it now.

00:05:29.551 --> 00:05:32.392
And then I'll come back
up and do some demos.

00:05:32.392 --> 00:05:33.600
ANDY GLOVER: Thank you, Matt.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:34.440
Thanks a lot.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.180
It's great to be here.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.650
Is everyone warm enough?

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:40.300
I'm freezing.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.100
So Jez Humble, who
wrote a seminal book

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:44.660
on continuous
delivery, captures what

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:47.290
I think is the essence
of continuous delivery.

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.870
And that's about making
deployments boring.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:54.070
No longer are they
cross-functional scheduled

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.950
events that require
a whole lot of people

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.330
to get in the same room and
figure out what's going on.

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:02.200
They're just simply done,
and they're done reliably.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.000
And the businesses that
are taking advantage

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.010
of continuous
delivery are obviously

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:10.384
seeing accelerated
time to market.

00:06:10.384 --> 00:06:12.300
They can produce products
quickly and get them

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.084
in front of customers quickly.

00:06:14.084 --> 00:06:15.500
And because they
can do that, they

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.030
turn out to build
the right product.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:19.530
They're not waiting a long time
to figure out whether or not

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:22.530
this product actually is
a viable thing for users.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:25.680
By delivering the
right product quickly,

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.290
they're seeing increased
customer satisfaction,

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:34.880
and they're delivering a product
that's of higher quality.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:39.590
And so continuous delivery
isn't new to Netflix.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:41.320
We've been doing it a long time.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:43.370
And in fact we
continuous delivery

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:48.850
as the facilitator for
innovation at Netflix.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:51.440
We went to the
cloud six years ago

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.120
and immediately saw that we need
to start building some tooling

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.460
so that we could start
pushing out software quickly

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:57.740
to you all.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:00.400
And we start with a
tool called Asgard.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.010
And Asgard, in
many ways, captured

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:09.150
the cloud model for Netflix and
maybe the industry in general

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:12.550
in terms of, there's the
notion of an application.

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:15.820
And an application has clusters.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:19.110
And clusters are made up of
autoscaling groups or server

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:20.840
groups.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:25.750
Asgard served us well and served
many other companies as well.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:29.330
And we did those see
that Asgard lacks

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:30.830
some of the real
continuous delivery

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:31.830
features that we needed.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.800
So we embarked on
another effort.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.080
And that effort
was called Mimir.

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:41.620
And Mimir added the notion of
pipelines in front of Asgard,

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:45.830
and thus created a bit
more velocity with respect

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:48.720
to how we are doing deployments.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.270
Around the same time
Mimir was built,

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.700
there was yet another continuous
delivery platform built

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:55.750
called Edge Center, which
the Netflix Edge Platform

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:58.820
team had built. And this
was also highly successful

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:03.960
in terms of rolling out
production software to the edge

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:06.520
of the Netflix architecture.

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:10.450
So continuous delivery
isn't new to Netflix.

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:12.000
We've been doing
this a long time.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.870
We've learned a heck
of a lot of lessons.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:15.730
We sure know how not to do it.

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:19.080
And we think we're pretty
good at knowing how to do it.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.100
And so about a year
and a half ago,

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:25.260
we embarked on a new effort to
create an entirely new platform

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:28.090
from the ground up
called Spinnaker.

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.930
And Spinnaker is a hardened,
continuous delivery platform

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:34.300
built to be tier zero for
the business of delivering

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:34.800
software.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:40.039
As I said, it's
leveraged the lessons

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:42.770
we've been learning for quite
a long time about how to move

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:45.630
quickly into the AWS cloud.

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:49.550
We open sourced Spinnaker
late last November.

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:53.990
And Spinnaker itself is
ultimately a workflow tool.

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:57.030
It has the notion of pipelines.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.460
Pipelines are made up of stages.

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:02.300
After you deploy an
asset to some endpoint,

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:05.450
you also have the ability
to manage those assets.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:07.580
And Spinnaker itself
integrates deeply

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:09.350
with other operational
systems that

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:13.390
can support telemetry, testing,
and performance monitoring

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:13.980
for example.

00:09:17.382 --> 00:09:22.200
And Spinnaker is built-- has
been built in partnership

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.310
with a number of companies,
including Google, Microsoft,

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.600
Pivotal, and Kenzan.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:31.310
And these partnerships
reflect a deep integration

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:33.850
with various systems, i.e.

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:39.360
Google Cloud Platform, Microsoft
Azure, Pivotal Cloud Foundry.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:45.440
And these partnerships
have been bidirectional

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.120
from the standpoint of,
we're learning from Google.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.130
We're learning from Azure.

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:53.126
And hopefully vice
versa, they're

00:09:53.126 --> 00:09:54.250
learning something from us.

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:59.920
And pluggability is
key to Spinnaker.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:01.570
Not only are the
assets that you can

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:04.970
deploy with Spinnaker
pluggable-- Netflix has

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:11.560
a very long history of
deploying AMIs, virtual images,

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:12.850
virtual machine images.

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:16.110
But we want to be able
to deploy other things.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:19.400
Stages of pipelines, they
themselves are also pluggable.

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:22.030
We have our own set of
stages internal to Netflix.

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:24.130
Some of them are open
source, some of them aren't.

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:25.990
Ostensibly other
companies will create

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:29.560
stages that are unique
to their environment.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:32.332
And ultimately the whole notion
of cloud providers themselves

00:10:32.332 --> 00:10:32.915
are pluggable.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:38.450
And this pluggability
is key for Netflix.

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:40.720
We want to leverage all of AWS.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.660
Previous tooling was
very much centered

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.870
around EC2 Compute or Compute.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:47.710
We want to take advantage
of other services

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:53.720
at AWS like ECS, S3,
CloudFront, Lambda.

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:57.170
All of AWS is a fertile ground
for Netflix deployments,

00:10:57.170 --> 00:10:59.950
and we want our tooling
to support it natively.

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:02.370
We also want to take
advantage of CDN deployments.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:05.790
Obviously Netflix has a very,
very large CDN presence.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.140
And we want to do-- we
want Spinnaker to handle

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:11.070
the deployments to Akamai.

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:15.030
Jars, libs, there are plenty
of teams that their end

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:17.530
asset necessarily isn't a
service that facilitates

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:19.970
watching video, but it
could be some library that

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:22.090
facilitates it some other way.

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:25.290
We want to be able to support
deploying that, that library

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.630
into like Artifactory
for example.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:31.240
And then last but
not least, the system

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:33.560
needs to be flexible
enough to handle the coming

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:35.265
tidal wave of containers.

00:11:35.265 --> 00:11:37.940
And the good news is that
Spinnaker supports all of these

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.100
through a pluggable model.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:45.160
As I said, we began Spinnaker
about a year and a half ago.

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:48.200
It's in production
today at Netflix.

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:51.890
And the success of
Spinnaker is predicated

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:53.750
on that it's a self
service platform.

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:56.650
It's got a super easy to
use UI which you'll see,

00:11:56.650 --> 00:11:58.810
that teams can go in
and figure out how

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:00.199
to start using it immediately.

00:12:00.199 --> 00:12:01.240
They don't need training.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:05.750
They don't need some
document or manual.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:09.350
The ability for teams to fashion
their own delivery pipelines--

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:12.290
there is no standard
pipeline at Netflix.

00:12:12.290 --> 00:12:14.230
There's a tool,
obviously Spinnaker,

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.920
that allows you to create
pipelines with various stages.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:19.170
But the stages that a
team decides to build

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:20.940
is entirely up to that team.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.340
So a wide breadth of pipelines.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:26.920
And then last but not
least, because Spinnaker

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:28.750
is open source, we
get to take advantage

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:30.620
of some of the
innovations that you all

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:33.360
are building in your
companies for your needs.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.859
And we've seen that already
today in that pull requests

00:12:35.859 --> 00:12:38.150
are coming in, and Netflix
is taking advantage of that.

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:39.750
So it's definitely a
bi-directional relationship.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.791
And that's why we choose
to open source software.

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:48.060
Today, Spinnaker is doing
4,000 deployments a day.

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:52.010
Netflix has roughly
two dozen AWS accounts

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:55.810
with a dozen being
accounts that are

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:59.000
important for streaming video.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:01.740
Because we have a
standard platform,

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:03.750
people can plug into it easily.

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:06.260
It has a flexibility
with pipelines.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:09.877
More and more teams are creating
pipelines day in and day out.

00:13:09.877 --> 00:13:11.710
And that's leading to
a skyrocketing number.

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:13.925
In fact, before I made
this presentation,

00:13:13.925 --> 00:13:15.350
I thought the number was 2,000.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:17.600
And then our data science
people actually corrected me

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:19.090
and said no, it's
actually 4,000.

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:22.710
So we've seen a tremendous
growth in deployments to AWS

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:25.385
within Netflix since
releasing Spinnaker.

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:32.845
And the beauty of this
pluggable-- yes, there it

00:13:32.845 --> 00:13:35.970
is-- the beauty of the
standard platform that's

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:38.360
pluggable is that
everyone at Netflix

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.430
benefits from Spinnaker.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:43.000
When new tools get online,
the first thing they do

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.520
is they integrate
with Spinnaker.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:46.610
And then all teams at
Netflix get advantage of it.

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:52.569
So case in point, we have
a-- a sophisticated-- I

00:13:52.569 --> 00:13:54.360
don't have enough coffee
and it's freezing,

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:57.130
so that's my excuse.

00:13:57.130 --> 00:14:00.830
We have a sophisticated
automated canary analysis

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:02.170
framework.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:03.990
That team plugged
it into Spinnaker.

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:05.800
It's a custom stage.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:07.960
And now everyone at
Netflix get to take

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:11.710
advantage of ACA, a
sophisticated form of testing

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:13.290
before you go live.

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:16.530
Another case in point,
Netflix has a container cloud

00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:19.600
called Titus for, obviously,
running containers.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:20.920
That's plugged into Spinnaker.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:25.170
Now all teams at Netflix can
leverage Titus for deployments

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:26.690
should they choose to do so.

00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:29.670
And as new tools and services
are built at Netflix,

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:32.170
those teams are
plugging into Spinnaker.

00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:33.900
And all teams at
Netflix again get

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:35.415
to leverage that innovation.

00:14:38.802 --> 00:14:41.010
So I said it was a beautiful
UI and it's easy to use.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.759
And again, Matt will
give more details.

00:14:43.759 --> 00:14:44.800
But it's a phenomenal UI.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:46.540
It's awesome.

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:50.300
And as I said, it allows you
to manage assets in production.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:51.800
So there's this
notion of a cluster.

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:53.750
A cluster spans the globe.

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:57.720
And then you have server groups
or ASGs inside of a cluster.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:00.870
In this case, here's a
server group that's being--

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:05.130
it's in US East in Amazon,
and then there's US West.

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:07.770
We codify deployment
patterns, again,

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:10.107
that umbrella of innovation.

00:15:10.107 --> 00:15:12.190
Red-black deployments,
which the rest of the world

00:15:12.190 --> 00:15:14.054
calls blue-green.

00:15:14.054 --> 00:15:15.470
All teams get to
take advantage of

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:20.460
these sophisticated deployment
strategies within Spinnaker.

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:23.930
Pipelines-- this is
the pipeline screen.

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:26.070
This is a pipeline
execution that

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:29.050
shows a series of stages
that ultimately culminates

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:31.550
in a deployment into production.

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:33.327
Spinnaker supports
parallel stages,

00:15:33.327 --> 00:15:35.160
so you can obviously
run things in parallel.

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.880
You can deploy to many regions
at once if you choose to.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:39.776
You have deployment
windows so you

00:15:39.776 --> 00:15:41.150
can elect to say
I want to deploy

00:15:41.150 --> 00:15:44.310
this particular asset at this
part of the globe at this time

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:46.560
of the day.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:50.150
All in all, it's a very
flexible, pluggable system.

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:54.170
And it's seen
phenomenal success--

00:15:54.170 --> 00:16:00.480
I'm having a hard time
here-- success at Netflix.

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:02.230
So I'd like to transition
it back to Matt.

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:04.063
We've been working--
Netflix and Google have

00:16:04.063 --> 00:16:06.380
been working together on
Spinnaker for about a year.

00:16:06.380 --> 00:16:09.650
And as I say, the innovation
is going both ways.

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:12.150
I'm going to assume you guys
have learned something from us.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:12.930
MATT DUFTLER: We hope so.

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:14.040
ANDY GLOVER: And we've
definitely learned something

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:16.387
from the Google folks
because obviously Google has

00:16:16.387 --> 00:16:18.220
a very interesting
infrastructure and belief

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:18.719
pattern.

00:16:18.719 --> 00:16:20.320
So it's been a
fruitful relationship.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:21.460
So I'm happy to be here.

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:23.110
MATT DUFTLER: Thanks, Andy.

00:16:23.110 --> 00:16:25.187
OK, time flies.

00:16:25.187 --> 00:16:26.270
It's been a year and half.

00:16:26.270 --> 00:16:27.103
ANDY GLOVER: Oh, OK.

00:16:27.103 --> 00:16:28.560
MATT DUFTLER: And a half.

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:30.860
So this raises an
important question.

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:32.700
Why is Google
investing in Spinnaker?

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:34.950
We've been working on it
just about a year and a half.

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:36.500
We started with one person.

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:38.870
Now we have a quite
sizable team--

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:41.890
a half dozen or so
engineers, a program manager,

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:43.460
product manager, and so on.

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:47.202
So we are solidly
invested in this effort.

00:16:47.202 --> 00:16:48.410
There are the obvious things.

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:50.520
Our principles align,
putting these tools

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:52.560
in the hands of the
line developers,

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:56.110
traceability-- so who did
what, when, where, why--

00:16:56.110 --> 00:16:58.270
and relying entirely on
immutable infrastructure.

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:01.030
So these things align, but
there's more to it than that.

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:02.530
Google is actively
engaged in trying

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:04.440
to take things that
we do well internally,

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:06.589
things we've been doing
for a long time internally,

00:17:06.589 --> 00:17:09.050
and making them
available externally.

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:10.890
But it's not as simple
as kind of hitting

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:13.869
a giant red easy button and
just making everything public.

00:17:13.869 --> 00:17:16.050
We can't flip any
bit to just share all

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:18.060
of our internal infrastructure.

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:20.420
So where it makes sense
and where it's reasonable,

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:23.030
we're participating in open
source efforts like this one

00:17:23.030 --> 00:17:25.630
to try to build on things that
are out there that others have

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:28.250
contributed to, and
then get to a point

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:30.430
where we have these sorts
of internal capabilities

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:31.390
available externally.

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:35.510
In the case of Spinnaker,
Netflix is a recognized leader

00:17:35.510 --> 00:17:37.850
in the move to cloud, so
it made a lot of sense

00:17:37.850 --> 00:17:39.516
for us to get
involved with this.

00:17:39.516 --> 00:17:41.640
There are a few additional
benefits which might not

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:44.010
be immediately obvious.

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:46.500
As we've developed
Spinnaker, the Netflix guys

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:48.880
have been sort of battle
testing this thing internally.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:51.170
It's running in
production all the time.

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:54.967
So A, we can have a high
degree of confidence

00:17:54.967 --> 00:17:56.550
that we're not making
breaking changes

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:58.174
because they're
constantly testing this

00:17:58.174 --> 00:18:00.640
and applying a
significant load to it.

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:04.525
And B, it brings some sort
of common sanity checks

00:18:04.525 --> 00:18:06.330
to any kinds of
changes we propose.

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:08.790
We're not just doing
things sort of as we feel.

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:09.790
We're discussing things.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:11.090
We're working out in the open.

00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:14.740
And then we all agree on
changes as we make them.

00:18:14.740 --> 00:18:16.940
And the final thing
worth mentioning

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:19.080
is that the end result is
a product for our users

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:20.710
and our customers
that doesn't rely

00:18:20.710 --> 00:18:22.790
on locking into our platform.

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:25.805
It works with a significant
set of cloud platforms.

00:18:25.805 --> 00:18:28.260
It works very well with GCE.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:31.900
We'll talk more about
integrations in the container

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:32.942
world.

00:18:32.942 --> 00:18:34.650
But it supports multiple
cloud platforms.

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:35.450
You're not locked in.

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:37.324
The capabilities are
quite similar across all

00:18:37.324 --> 00:18:39.690
the different platforms.

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:42.050
So let's leave that there.

00:18:42.050 --> 00:18:44.190
In one second, we're
going to do a demo.

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:51.870
OK, can I have the
demo laptop please?

00:18:56.530 --> 00:18:58.780
I'll kick this off and then
explain what we're seeing.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:17.195
So this is a very
simple web application.

00:19:17.195 --> 00:19:18.570
I'm going to make
a simple change

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:20.970
so we can see that
change reflected,

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:23.080
and then I'll explain in
detail what's going on.

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:29.720
I changed one local file.

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:32.010
I'm going to change
a version number.

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:33.430
And then I'll explain each step.

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:36.585
I just want to give
it time to get going.

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:55.720
So I have a local git repo.

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:57.570
It has a very simple
web application in it.

00:19:57.570 --> 00:19:58.930
It's a typical hello world.

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:00.410
Whatever parameter
I pass to it, it

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:02.660
will echo it back and
put hello in front of it.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:03.500
I made some change.

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:05.360
I changed it from
saying hello to saying

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:08.900
hello GCP Next, and then
whatever name we pass to it.

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:11.660
And then I did a local commit.

00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:13.140
It's a simple application.

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:15.330
But we have Jenkins set
up to poll every minute

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:16.760
or two on that local Git repo.

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:18.270
And when it notices
any change was

00:20:18.270 --> 00:20:21.600
pushed to that branch, which
in this case is just master,

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:23.030
it'll kick off a build.

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:27.480
And that build uses Gradle,
packages up that application,

00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:29.840
and then produces a deb
package and publishes it.

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:32.590
And we'll look at each of
these steps in more detail.

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:35.050
This particular web
application wasn't built by us.

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:37.750
It was built by Kenzan Media,
one of the other participants

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:40.580
in the open source effort.

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:43.030
So we could see Jenkins
picked up that change

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:46.347
and executed this
build, build number 25.

00:20:46.347 --> 00:20:48.930
So at this point-- can we have
the presentation laptop please?

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:55.436
So this gives a kind
of conceptual overview

00:20:55.436 --> 00:20:57.310
of the different components
at work, and also

00:20:57.310 --> 00:20:59.580
the workflow if we read
it from left to right.

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:01.050
So I'm going to
start at the left.

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:02.675
I'll explain what I
did so far and then

00:21:02.675 --> 00:21:03.700
what we're going to see.

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:07.300
And I'm going to try to talk
slowly, which I'm not great at.

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:10.720
But the idea is I want to
let the bake stage catch up.

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:12.630
So starting at the left,
I pushed the commit.

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:13.796
I made a very simple change.

00:21:13.796 --> 00:21:16.400
We'll be able to see that
change reflected and tested

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:17.430
in production.

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:20.350
And then I pushed that
change to the local Git repo.

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:22.800
Jenkins picked up the
change through its polling,

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:25.860
builds that web application,
produces an OS package--

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:28.860
in this case, a Debian archive--
and then publishes that

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:29.906
to a repo.

00:21:29.906 --> 00:21:31.280
In practice this
can be something

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:32.445
like Bintray, Artifactory.

00:21:32.445 --> 00:21:34.180
You have lots of choices.

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:39.050
For the demo, I'm running
a local actually deb repo.

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:41.790
This is a point where a
typical continuous integration

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:44.120
system like Jenkins and
a continuous delivery

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:47.140
framework like Spinnaker
start to intersect.

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:49.390
So we're right now where
the blue shading begins

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:52.120
to the left of that bake box.

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:54.220
So the stuff in the
blue shaded area,

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:58.290
this represents a set of
Spinnaker stages wired together

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:00.120
in a Spinnaker pipeline.

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:02.720
And this is where immutable
infrastructure comes into play.

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:05.660
So when we push a
change, our CI system

00:22:05.660 --> 00:22:07.300
picked it up, built
it, ostensibly

00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:10.080
ran unit tests-- although
I don't have any-- produced

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:12.500
a deb package, published
a deb package to some well

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:14.044
known repo.

00:22:14.044 --> 00:22:14.960
We have an option now.

00:22:14.960 --> 00:22:16.660
We could shell into
our running systems,

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:18.740
upgrade that
application in place,

00:22:18.740 --> 00:22:20.490
and then we have the
new version deployed.

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:23.031
But that wouldn't be adhering
to the immutable infrastructure

00:22:23.031 --> 00:22:23.796
principles.

00:22:23.796 --> 00:22:25.170
So what we're
going to do instead

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:27.889
is take a static image, a
hermetically sealed image,

00:22:27.889 --> 00:22:30.180
which should be familiar if
you're familiar with Docker

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:32.410
and containerization efforts.

00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:34.180
In this case it's a VM image.

00:22:34.180 --> 00:22:35.734
We'll package up that image.

00:22:35.734 --> 00:22:38.150
Then we're going to deploy
brand new cloud infrastructure,

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:40.260
so cloud VMs with that image.

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:41.760
We'll test those images.

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:43.700
Once we decide
they're happy, then we

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:47.160
can disable or destroy
the old servers.

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:48.312
So why disable or destroy?

00:22:48.312 --> 00:22:49.770
If we're done with
the old ones, we

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:51.430
can just destroy them, right?

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:54.410
The idea with disable is its a
traffic management operation.

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:56.677
Instead of just destroying
your old servers,

00:22:56.677 --> 00:22:59.010
just arrange it so that they
don't take any more traffic

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:01.060
from the load balancer
sitting in front of them.

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:05.110
This way if something goes
wrong, you can quickly go back.

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:06.414
So make a change.

00:23:06.414 --> 00:23:07.080
Push the change.

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:08.240
Build a deb package.

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:10.510
Produce a static machine image.

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:13.600
Deploy that static machine
image into a test cluster,

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:15.360
so a new set of VMs.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:17.120
Wait until things
are deemed healthy.

00:23:17.120 --> 00:23:19.651
And then disable or destroy
the old server group.

00:23:19.651 --> 00:23:21.150
And there's a little
bit more to it.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:23.274
If you look at the top and
bottom-- so we mentioned

00:23:23.274 --> 00:23:25.100
it works in multiple
cloud platforms.

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:27.300
The test here is
running against GCE,

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:29.230
so that's really the top half.

00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:31.690
So we're producing
a GCE machine image,

00:23:31.690 --> 00:23:34.280
and then we're deploying a
GCE managed instance group.

00:23:34.280 --> 00:23:36.440
In Spinnaker terminology,
we call these groups

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:38.660
of homogeneous VM server groups.

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:41.700
But it's a managed
instance group on GCE.

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:44.740
If we were deploying an
Amazon EC2 server group,

00:23:44.740 --> 00:23:47.680
it would be an
autoscaling group.

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:48.280
Make sense?

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:54.570
So this is what
we're doing, right?

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:55.110
Build code.

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:55.610
Test it.

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:57.610
When you're happy, push
it in through the stages

00:23:57.610 --> 00:23:59.720
into production.

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:01.310
So we're taking all
these first class

00:24:01.310 --> 00:24:04.945
operations, this idea of a bake,
a deploy, disable, destroy.

00:24:04.945 --> 00:24:06.820
We're taking all these
things, and then we're

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:08.390
building a set of pipelines.

00:24:08.390 --> 00:24:10.770
And we're using that
set of pipelines

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:13.260
to have a system that
takes source code changes

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:15.850
and pushes them all the
way through to production

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:17.790
in a disciplined
way and leaves open

00:24:17.790 --> 00:24:20.110
the possibility of doing
a rollback very easily

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:21.700
and quickly.

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:24.510
So we roughly described
the first pipeline.

00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:25.840
So we bake, deploy.

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.120
And in this case, if
you're just doing a test,

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:29.230
you can do something
like a destroy.

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:30.813
You're not going to
do a rollback back

00:24:30.813 --> 00:24:31.760
in a test cluster.

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:33.614
One thing worth noting
here, we mentioned

00:24:33.614 --> 00:24:35.530
we don't have it in mind
to rebuild everything

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:36.120
from scratch.

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:38.230
We're trying to rely on
existing open source efforts

00:24:38.230 --> 00:24:40.230
where it makes sense and
build on things that we

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:42.030
consider the best of breed.

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:45.210
The bakery relies entirely
on Packer from HashiCorp.

00:24:45.210 --> 00:24:46.910
We built a web
service around it.

00:24:46.910 --> 00:24:49.330
But basically it's a
wrapper around Packer

00:24:49.330 --> 00:24:51.800
that understands naming
conventions and versioning

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:53.880
and things that are
Spinnaker specific,

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.810
but then delegates the actual
work of producing those machine

00:24:56.810 --> 00:24:58.130
images to Packer.

00:24:58.130 --> 00:25:00.322
So we didn't reinvent
baking machine images.

00:25:00.322 --> 00:25:01.780
We're just wrapping
it in something

00:25:01.780 --> 00:25:04.922
that makes it a proper Spinnaker
microservice and introduces

00:25:04.922 --> 00:25:06.380
versioning and all
the other things

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:09.800
we need for it to
behave properly.

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:12.466
So once that's done, it's going
to trigger another pipeline.

00:25:12.466 --> 00:25:14.090
And that triggering
bit is interesting.

00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:15.080
If you look all
the way left, you

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:16.660
see that sort of Jenkins guy.

00:25:16.660 --> 00:25:18.510
And there's a
picture from Docker.

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:20.580
The idea is you can
kick off pipelines

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:22.460
with a choice of triggers.

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:24.575
There are Cron based
triggers and other things.

00:25:24.575 --> 00:25:26.120
But once that
pipeline completes,

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:27.661
we want another
pipeline to kick off.

00:25:27.661 --> 00:25:29.400
So that's a pipeline trigger.

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:31.310
So the first one
was a code push.

00:25:31.310 --> 00:25:33.012
That pipeline
complete successfully.

00:25:33.012 --> 00:25:35.470
Then we have a pipeline trigger
to kick off this validation

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:37.630
pipeline.

00:25:37.630 --> 00:25:39.656
For the purposes of
the demo, the pipeline

00:25:39.656 --> 00:25:41.280
that does validation
does nothing other

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:43.580
than have a manual
judgment stage that says

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:44.830
validate everything's working.

00:25:44.830 --> 00:25:47.400
And then we'll go and click,
yep, everything's good.

00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:49.480
Once that pipeline
completes successfully,

00:25:49.480 --> 00:25:52.362
we have one final pipeline, and
this is the promotion pipeline.

00:25:52.362 --> 00:25:54.570
And this is where it really
gets kind of interesting.

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:56.028
So what we've done
to this point is

00:25:56.028 --> 00:25:58.710
push a code change
via a deb package

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:01.101
onto a new set of
machines into testing.

00:26:01.101 --> 00:26:03.100
Once we're happy with
that image-- and remember,

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:05.860
it's not the servers we're
testing, it's the image.

00:26:05.860 --> 00:26:08.450
Once we're happy that the
tests have passed successfully,

00:26:08.450 --> 00:26:10.899
we want to deploy that exact
same image into production.

00:26:10.899 --> 00:26:12.440
We don't want to
produce a new image.

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:15.290
We don't want to reinstall
that deb package.

00:26:15.290 --> 00:26:17.260
We don't want to open
ourselves up to the risk

00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:19.067
that one of the
systems we depend on

00:26:19.067 --> 00:26:21.650
has maybe increased its version
or we get a later version that

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:25.400
has some protocols switched off
by default. That's Cassandra.

00:26:25.400 --> 00:26:27.700
So we don't want to open
ourselves up to that.

00:26:27.700 --> 00:26:30.060
We want to push that
image that we've tested

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:31.910
into the production cluster.

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:33.660
So at that point,
instead of a bake stage,

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:34.790
we use to find image stage.

00:26:34.790 --> 00:26:35.956
And you give it coordinates.

00:26:35.956 --> 00:26:37.530
You say, go to this cluster.

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:39.650
Find the latest or the
largest server group,

00:26:39.650 --> 00:26:41.910
whatever makes sense
for your deployment.

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:43.470
Gather those image
details and make

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:45.660
them available in the
context of that pipeline.

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:47.900
So then any
downstream deploy uses

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:50.231
that image for its deployment.

00:26:50.231 --> 00:26:52.730
And finally, if you look at the
end of that last deployment,

00:26:52.730 --> 00:26:53.990
we have a deployment strategy.

00:26:53.990 --> 00:26:54.990
So this is a blue-green.

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:57.160
The Netflix guys
with patriotism call

00:26:57.160 --> 00:26:59.140
these red-black deployments.

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:00.929
Most people call
these blue-green.

00:27:00.929 --> 00:27:03.470
So what it's going to do is not
destroy the old server group.

00:27:03.470 --> 00:27:05.803
We said in testing fine, we'll
get rid of the old server

00:27:05.803 --> 00:27:07.170
group, no big deal.

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:08.630
But in production,
we're just going

00:27:08.630 --> 00:27:10.820
to make it so that the older
server group, the older

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:13.237
version of the software
deployed on the old VMs,

00:27:13.237 --> 00:27:14.695
just doesn't take
any more traffic.

00:27:14.695 --> 00:27:15.630
It still exists.

00:27:15.630 --> 00:27:16.780
We don't destroy it.

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:19.220
But it gets deregistered
from its load balancers.

00:27:19.220 --> 00:27:21.636
And then if something goes
wrong, we can quickly rollback.

00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:26.570
Can I have the
demo laptop please?

00:27:34.450 --> 00:27:36.230
We mentioned the
three pipelines.

00:27:36.230 --> 00:27:38.950
So bake and deploy to
test is the top one.

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:41.670
The middle one is validate test.

00:27:41.670 --> 00:27:43.770
And the last is
promote to production.

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:45.910
So this top pipeline
is executing.

00:27:45.910 --> 00:27:47.960
So we had the bake stage.

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:49.710
There's the usual
attributes you'd expect,

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:51.127
what distro to use.

00:27:51.127 --> 00:27:53.460
It describes what package is
being installed, what cloud

00:27:53.460 --> 00:27:56.040
platform is being targeted.

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:59.229
And that completed successfully,
took about four minutes.

00:27:59.229 --> 00:28:00.520
Then we had a deploy operation.

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:05.210
Could render better.

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:07.000
Resolution's a little weird.

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:09.750
So we're deploying that image
that we baked in the bake stage

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:10.696
into the test cluster.

00:28:10.696 --> 00:28:12.320
So again, you just
give it coordinates.

00:28:12.320 --> 00:28:16.470
In this case, it's GCP
next-test is the test cluster.

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:18.800
And then following that
is the destroy operation.

00:28:18.800 --> 00:28:21.240
And what the destroy
operation will do in this case

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:22.741
is determine the
target server group

00:28:22.741 --> 00:28:24.240
based on the
coordinates, which will

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:26.870
be just the previous server
group or the older version.

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:28.020
And then it destroys it.

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:31.060
And if we take a look at the
clusters view-- and this is

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:34.336
very much like what Andy
showed, only instead of 600

00:28:34.336 --> 00:28:37.250
and a thousand VMs
in each server group,

00:28:37.250 --> 00:28:40.510
we really went for it and
have one in each server group.

00:28:40.510 --> 00:28:43.840
So I have no backup video,
so I'm playing it safe.

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:46.720
So this is a test
cluster on the bottom.

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:52.929
And if we look here, this is
the newly deployed server group.

00:28:52.929 --> 00:28:54.970
And when you click on any
of these server groups,

00:28:54.970 --> 00:28:56.200
you can drill down
into the details.

00:28:56.200 --> 00:28:57.110
And where it gets
very interesting

00:28:57.110 --> 00:28:59.100
is you start to see
the image information.

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:01.486
This is the image
that's under test.

00:29:01.486 --> 00:29:03.735
That's the image that was
produced by that bake stage.

00:29:03.735 --> 00:29:06.390
You can see it in the execution
details of the bake stage.

00:29:06.390 --> 00:29:09.064
And that's the image that we
push into the test cluster.

00:29:09.064 --> 00:29:10.480
So we now have a
new server group.

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:12.480
And you can see the old
server group disappeared

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:13.350
while I was talking.

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:15.990
So that was that destroy stage.

00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:17.900
And the idea is we
would test this.

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:20.739
So one thing we can
do to test this,

00:29:20.739 --> 00:29:22.155
we can click on
its load balancer.

00:29:25.170 --> 00:29:29.485
Capture the DNS name or the IP
address of the load balancer.

00:29:29.485 --> 00:29:30.860
And that's the
change we've made.

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:32.865
We made it say
hello from GCP Next.

00:29:32.865 --> 00:29:34.156
I don't know if that's legible.

00:29:36.879 --> 00:29:38.670
If we look at the
production cluster that's

00:29:38.670 --> 00:29:41.990
running above it, which is this
one, and we do the same thing.

00:29:41.990 --> 00:29:46.480
I have them associated with
different load balancers.

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:49.880
So we click on the IP address
for the production load

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:52.470
balancer.

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:54.000
It has the old style message.

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:56.060
So it doesn't have that change.

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:58.790
So ostensibly I've done more
testing than just click on that

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:00.219
and put my name in.

00:30:00.219 --> 00:30:02.635
But we're still going to go
into the manual judgment stage

00:30:02.635 --> 00:30:04.010
and said yes,
we've validated it.

00:30:04.010 --> 00:30:07.080
We're happy with the behavior
of that image under test.

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:08.580
At this point it's
going to kick off

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:11.610
one final pipeline, which
is a promotion to production

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:12.510
pipeline.

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:14.340
And the first stage
here is find image.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:16.000
And what this does
is look at that

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:19.080
cluster, which is
the test cluster,

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:20.370
determine what image to use.

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:22.300
And then it primes the
context of the pipeline

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:23.210
with that image.

00:30:23.210 --> 00:30:24.126
And this is important.

00:30:24.126 --> 00:30:26.932
When you configure a deploy
stage within a pipeline,

00:30:26.932 --> 00:30:28.390
you don't put in
image information.

00:30:28.390 --> 00:30:31.110
The image information
comes from context.

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:33.400
So if it's something that
was triggered directly

00:30:33.400 --> 00:30:36.190
by a Jenkins job
completing, the image

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:40.121
that gets primed into context
is associated with-- I'm sorry.

00:30:40.121 --> 00:30:42.370
If you have a pipeline that's
triggered from a Jenkins

00:30:42.370 --> 00:30:44.040
context and you have
a bake, the bake

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:46.220
produces an image
using the artifacts

00:30:46.220 --> 00:30:48.450
produced from that Jenkins job.

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:50.100
And then the deployment
uses the image

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:52.140
that was produced
from the bake stage.

00:30:52.140 --> 00:30:53.892
So you never say use this image.

00:30:53.892 --> 00:30:56.100
When you have another pipeline
that has a find image,

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:57.990
it looks in the
existing cluster,

00:30:57.990 --> 00:30:59.570
determines what
image was deployed,

00:30:59.570 --> 00:31:01.480
and then primes the
context with that image.

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:03.780
And that can be used by
downstream deployments.

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:05.740
But it's important that you're
never explicitly selecting

00:31:05.740 --> 00:31:07.740
an image by name to use
in one of the pipelines.

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:10.790
It's always from context.

00:31:10.790 --> 00:31:12.930
So while that's running,
we'll go back here,

00:31:12.930 --> 00:31:14.210
and we'll see what's going on.

00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:17.550
So prior to kicking off
that final pipeline,

00:31:17.550 --> 00:31:20.320
we had two server groups
in the production cluster.

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:22.250
So we had six and seven.

00:31:22.250 --> 00:31:23.880
So six is now grayed out.

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:25.050
So it's disabled.

00:31:25.050 --> 00:31:26.500
Seven is still enabled.

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:27.510
Eight is coming online.

00:31:27.510 --> 00:31:31.220
So the server group exists,
but has zero VMs deployed.

00:31:31.220 --> 00:31:32.939
So it's still spinning up.

00:31:32.939 --> 00:31:35.230
And what it's going to do
when it does this deployment,

00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:36.813
we said there's a
deployment strategy,

00:31:36.813 --> 00:31:38.914
this blue-green
deployment strategy.

00:31:38.914 --> 00:31:40.580
It's going to take
the old server group,

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:43.240
disable it so it doesn't
take any more traffic,

00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:45.950
after the new one has
already reported healthy.

00:31:45.950 --> 00:31:48.080
So deploy the new one,
wait for it's healthy,

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:50.470
disable the old one so it's
disassociated from its load

00:31:50.470 --> 00:31:51.855
balancers.

00:31:51.855 --> 00:31:53.230
Then it will do
a bit of cleanup.

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:54.870
And in this case,
it'll make sure there

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:56.703
are no more than two
server groups deployed.

00:31:56.703 --> 00:32:00.470
So it'll go and destroy any
old disabled server groups

00:32:00.470 --> 00:32:03.870
beyond the one that was
immediately disabled.

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:07.220
That all making sense?

00:32:07.220 --> 00:32:09.326
So we'll let that
run, and then--

00:32:14.110 --> 00:32:17.835
So we described
immutable infrastructure.

00:32:17.835 --> 00:32:19.960
We described some of the
capabilities of Spinnaker,

00:32:19.960 --> 00:32:21.626
some of these sort
of first class stages

00:32:21.626 --> 00:32:23.930
like bake, deploy,
disable, destroy.

00:32:23.930 --> 00:32:26.800
There are the other things you'd
expect, like resize, enable.

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:28.020
You can reenable something.

00:32:28.020 --> 00:32:28.728
There's rollback.

00:32:28.728 --> 00:32:30.060
There's a host of capabilities.

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:32.400
All the things you see
available in the pipelines

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:34.150
are really built on
top of operations

00:32:34.150 --> 00:32:36.840
that are available in kind of
an ad hoc form within the UI.

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:38.540
So you can do troubleshooting.

00:32:38.540 --> 00:32:40.600
You can isolate VMs
that aren't behaving

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:42.590
and take them out
of the group so they

00:32:42.590 --> 00:32:44.162
don't take further traffic.

00:32:44.162 --> 00:32:45.786
But the important
thing to keep in mind

00:32:45.786 --> 00:32:48.285
is that these principles, while
we've demonstrated them here

00:32:48.285 --> 00:32:51.100
so far with virtual
machines, apply equally well

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:52.940
to a world built on containers.

00:32:52.940 --> 00:32:54.200
So we've been hard at work.

00:32:54.200 --> 00:32:56.910
And we have now an
integration with Kubernetes

00:32:56.910 --> 00:32:59.670
far enough along that I can
demonstrated it here live.

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:03.480
And again, no backup video,
so it could get real exciting.

00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:05.794
But the integration's
in quite solid shape.

00:33:05.794 --> 00:33:07.710
Everything I'm showing
you here is checked in.

00:33:07.710 --> 00:33:10.570
So I'm running what's
committed at head basically.

00:33:10.570 --> 00:33:12.190
So anything we're
doing here, you

00:33:12.190 --> 00:33:14.650
can easily try out on your own.

00:33:14.650 --> 00:33:16.120
So I'll kick off
the demo and then

00:33:16.120 --> 00:33:18.650
I'll explain a bit more about
the Kubernetes integration

00:33:18.650 --> 00:33:20.936
because I don't want
to run out of time.

00:33:24.340 --> 00:33:26.394
So I'll just get it
going then we'll--

00:33:30.457 --> 00:33:32.540
So this is another simple
hello world application.

00:33:32.540 --> 00:33:35.855
This time it's in Go
instead of Java or Groovy.

00:33:38.510 --> 00:33:40.572
So I'm changing
a version number.

00:33:40.572 --> 00:33:42.030
And then I have an
endpoint that'll

00:33:42.030 --> 00:33:44.150
let me see the version
when it comes up.

00:33:44.150 --> 00:33:47.136
So we'll be able to
determine that we actually

00:33:47.136 --> 00:33:48.135
got the correct version.

00:33:53.090 --> 00:33:54.350
So I changed one simple file.

00:34:06.167 --> 00:34:08.000
So I went into this
very simple application.

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:09.500
I changed the version
string that'll

00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:11.639
be returned from slash version.

00:34:11.639 --> 00:34:15.570
And then I basically tagged
and pushed that container image

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:17.050
to a Docker registry.

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:20.050
So in this case I'm running
using Google Container

00:34:20.050 --> 00:34:20.550
Registry.

00:34:20.550 --> 00:34:22.230
It's like a hosted
Docker registry,

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:24.630
makes it easy to
configure the credentials.

00:34:24.630 --> 00:34:26.620
But the import things
that all I did was just

00:34:26.620 --> 00:34:27.570
tag it and push it.

00:34:27.570 --> 00:34:30.290
I didn't do anything else.

00:34:30.290 --> 00:34:32.530
The integration between
Spinnaker and Kubernetes

00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:34.540
relies on a few
different things.

00:34:34.540 --> 00:34:37.290
The first, you need
a Kubernetes cluster.

00:34:37.290 --> 00:34:40.510
So in this case, as we get into
the demo, it will become clear.

00:34:40.510 --> 00:34:42.596
But we're running against
Google Container Engine.

00:34:42.596 --> 00:34:43.929
It just makes it easy to set up.

00:34:43.929 --> 00:34:45.860
But any Kubernetes
cluster that you have,

00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:49.414
whether it's on-prem
or on AWS or using GKE,

00:34:49.414 --> 00:34:50.580
it'll work exactly the same.

00:34:50.580 --> 00:34:53.770
You just add a cluster endpoint
and credentials to access it.

00:34:53.770 --> 00:34:56.159
So the integration needs
a Kubernetes cluster,

00:34:56.159 --> 00:34:58.200
credentials to access
it, and it needs

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:00.140
to know where to
pull images from.

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:02.630
For that sample application,
I'm using Google Container

00:35:02.630 --> 00:35:03.130
Registry.

00:35:03.130 --> 00:35:06.960
So I configured my Kubernetes
account within Spinnaker

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:09.710
to pull my Google
Container Registry account.

00:35:09.710 --> 00:35:11.820
So it's just looking
in those repositories.

00:35:11.820 --> 00:35:13.900
But what it's doing is
polling at some interval.

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:16.410
And any time it sees a
changed to any image,

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:18.144
it pumps an event
into Spinnaker.

00:35:18.144 --> 00:35:19.810
And then if I have a
pipeline configured

00:35:19.810 --> 00:35:23.600
to trigger from a change to any
image in my container registry,

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:24.995
it'll kick off that pipeline.

00:35:24.995 --> 00:35:26.620
And you can do the
things you'd expect.

00:35:26.620 --> 00:35:29.560
You can make it listen for
a specific tag or all tags.

00:35:29.560 --> 00:35:32.680
You can have it listen for
changes to multiple images.

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:34.289
All of that works quite well.

00:35:34.289 --> 00:35:35.830
In this case we're
keeping it simple.

00:35:35.830 --> 00:35:36.880
It's one image.

00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:40.307
It's all within my same
Google Cloud Platform account.

00:35:40.307 --> 00:35:41.890
And I'm relying on
the hosted services

00:35:41.890 --> 00:35:43.150
to make it a bit easier.

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:48.870
So I mentioned, there are
different kinds of triggers.

00:35:48.870 --> 00:35:50.640
We'll dig into
the pipeline here.

00:35:54.800 --> 00:35:57.130
So this Docker triggered
basically says,

00:35:57.130 --> 00:35:58.920
any push to a Docker
registry-- and that's

00:35:58.920 --> 00:36:01.950
the registry to
poll-- that involves

00:36:01.950 --> 00:36:03.790
a change to that
repository-- in this case,

00:36:03.790 --> 00:36:06.200
I didn't specify tag,
so any tagged image

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:07.470
will kick off this pipeline.

00:36:07.470 --> 00:36:11.140
If it detects that change,
it kicks off the pipeline.

00:36:11.140 --> 00:36:13.861
So what does the
pipeline look like?

00:36:13.861 --> 00:36:14.610
It's quite simple.

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:15.839
There's a deploy stage.

00:36:15.839 --> 00:36:17.130
And we didn't look at this yet.

00:36:17.130 --> 00:36:19.726
I could show you what the
Deploy Wizard looks like.

00:36:19.726 --> 00:36:21.600
This looks pretty similar
from cloud platform

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:22.350
to cloud platform.

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:24.610
The details differ a little
bit, but the overall flow

00:36:24.610 --> 00:36:27.814
is quite consistent
between platforms.

00:36:27.814 --> 00:36:29.980
It's not a pure abstraction
where every platform has

00:36:29.980 --> 00:36:32.080
to fit the exact shape,
but the experience

00:36:32.080 --> 00:36:34.264
is consistent from
platform to platform.

00:36:34.264 --> 00:36:35.930
So there's all the
details you'd expect.

00:36:35.930 --> 00:36:39.922
You have to choose what
containers you want to deploy.

00:36:39.922 --> 00:36:41.380
And it's just
polling the registry.

00:36:41.380 --> 00:36:43.046
So whatever's available
in your registry

00:36:43.046 --> 00:36:46.960
is what's available in the UI.

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:48.700
You can associate it
with load balancers.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:52.020
Now all of these constructs
map so the proper Kubernetes

00:36:52.020 --> 00:36:52.580
construct.

00:36:52.580 --> 00:36:55.350
So load balancers
map to services.

00:36:55.350 --> 00:36:56.530
There's server groups.

00:36:56.530 --> 00:36:59.020
And we explain on GCE, these
are managed instance groups.

00:36:59.020 --> 00:37:00.980
On AWS, there are
autoscaling groups.

00:37:00.980 --> 00:37:03.520
On Kubernetes, these are
replication controllers.

00:37:03.520 --> 00:37:06.260
And whereas on the different
virtual machine integrations

00:37:06.260 --> 00:37:09.500
we described each
server group deploys

00:37:09.500 --> 00:37:13.870
VMs that really contained a
single instance, the server

00:37:13.870 --> 00:37:15.610
groups in this case
are application

00:37:15.610 --> 00:37:17.190
controllers deploying
pods that could

00:37:17.190 --> 00:37:19.570
contain multiple containers.

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:21.340
So this demo, we're
just applying one.

00:37:21.340 --> 00:37:23.215
But you can deploy as
many containers as make

00:37:23.215 --> 00:37:26.040
sense for your pod.

00:37:26.040 --> 00:37:26.540
And so on.

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:28.956
And you can specify all the
resource settings, environment

00:37:28.956 --> 00:37:30.410
variables, mount volumes.

00:37:30.410 --> 00:37:33.530
All the things you'd expect
to be able to do, you can do.

00:37:33.530 --> 00:37:35.720
And the nice thing
about this is that we're

00:37:35.720 --> 00:37:38.062
providing a consistent
experience across platforms

00:37:38.062 --> 00:37:39.770
and we're hiding a
lot of the complexity.

00:37:39.770 --> 00:37:41.982
You can always drill down,
get to all the details.

00:37:41.982 --> 00:37:43.440
But you don't have
to bury yourself

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:44.440
in all that complexity.

00:37:44.440 --> 00:37:46.250
So your sort of day
to day workflows, you

00:37:46.250 --> 00:37:48.400
deploy your changes
into production

00:37:48.400 --> 00:37:50.870
is made quite a bit easier.

00:37:50.870 --> 00:37:53.540
So we have a deploy stage, and
then we have a disable server

00:37:53.540 --> 00:37:54.570
group stage.

00:37:54.570 --> 00:37:57.650
And in this case, all Kubernetes
relies on selectors or labels,

00:37:57.650 --> 00:37:59.240
so that's what we're doing.

00:37:59.240 --> 00:38:01.670
We're not disassociating
it from a load balancer.

00:38:01.670 --> 00:38:04.760
We're changing a label such
that the services selectors

00:38:04.760 --> 00:38:07.820
don't pick up those
replication controllers.

00:38:07.820 --> 00:38:10.620
And then again, a
manual judgment stage.

00:38:10.620 --> 00:38:12.770
And I've been just as
thorough with my testing

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:13.670
as I was before.

00:38:13.670 --> 00:38:14.580
It says validated.

00:38:14.580 --> 00:38:17.130
We want to validate
the test cluster.

00:38:17.130 --> 00:38:17.840
Same thing.

00:38:17.840 --> 00:38:19.690
And we'll just click
OK when we get to it.

00:38:19.690 --> 00:38:21.610
And then the last
thing is a resize.

00:38:21.610 --> 00:38:23.205
So let's go back.

00:38:23.205 --> 00:38:24.850
Let's see how far it got.

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:26.830
So it did the deploy.

00:38:26.830 --> 00:38:27.880
It did the disable.

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:29.540
And now it's waiting
for my judgment.

00:38:29.540 --> 00:38:31.290
I have very good judgment.

00:38:31.290 --> 00:38:32.630
I'll say it worked great.

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:33.920
Now it's doing a resize.

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:35.870
So let's take a look
at the cluster view

00:38:35.870 --> 00:38:38.250
while this kicks off.

00:38:38.250 --> 00:38:39.420
So this is the cluster.

00:38:39.420 --> 00:38:40.790
And I'll explain a
bit more about what's

00:38:40.790 --> 00:38:42.150
on the screen in a second,
because there's one

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:43.620
other thing I want to call out.

00:38:43.620 --> 00:38:45.869
But you can see that grayed
out cluster at the bottom.

00:38:45.869 --> 00:38:47.150
That was deployed previously.

00:38:47.150 --> 00:38:48.250
It was disabled.

00:38:48.250 --> 00:38:49.427
So it's grayed out.

00:38:49.427 --> 00:38:51.760
The one that's resizing now,
when it was first deployed,

00:38:51.760 --> 00:38:52.960
it had one container.

00:38:52.960 --> 00:38:54.050
We said test it.

00:38:54.050 --> 00:38:56.140
Once we're happy with the
test, we'll scale it up.

00:38:56.140 --> 00:38:58.297
In this case I scaled
it to 400 containers.

00:38:58.297 --> 00:38:59.880
And it'll take a
minute or so and then

00:38:59.880 --> 00:39:01.652
you'll see they'll
all turn green.

00:39:01.652 --> 00:39:03.860
But there's one other
interesting thing on this page.

00:39:03.860 --> 00:39:06.670
So at the bottom we have
a Kubernetes cluster.

00:39:06.670 --> 00:39:08.464
And in the middle we
have a GCE cluster.

00:39:08.464 --> 00:39:10.380
And then at the top we
have an Amazon cluster.

00:39:10.380 --> 00:39:11.713
And they all look quite similar.

00:39:11.713 --> 00:39:13.820
All the health checking
works quite similarly.

00:39:13.820 --> 00:39:16.180
Sort of the high level
view looks about the same.

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:17.954
But then as you
start to drill down,

00:39:17.954 --> 00:39:19.370
you can see there
are differences.

00:39:19.370 --> 00:39:21.329
And you can get to the
details if you want.

00:39:21.329 --> 00:39:23.370
So you can see obviously
the zones are different.

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:26.240
And you could see the VPC
information, and so on.

00:39:26.240 --> 00:39:28.700
And there are context
menus and things everywhere

00:39:28.700 --> 00:39:33.290
that makes sense for the
selected resource type.

00:39:33.290 --> 00:39:34.600
So that should be deployed.

00:39:34.600 --> 00:39:36.510
So this is cruising right along.

00:39:36.510 --> 00:39:37.630
OK, so it's done.

00:39:37.630 --> 00:39:39.580
The Kubernetes stuff
works very well.

00:39:39.580 --> 00:39:42.180
It's very fast, kind of snappy.

00:39:42.180 --> 00:39:44.734
And one nice thing is we
expose the YAML everywhere.

00:39:44.734 --> 00:39:46.650
So if you actually want
to see the YAML that's

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:48.850
backing all these
resources, it's quite easy

00:39:48.850 --> 00:39:50.774
to drill down on any one.

00:39:55.312 --> 00:39:57.270
If I could have the
presentation laptop please?

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:02.910
So you can see how it maps.

00:40:02.910 --> 00:40:04.324
We covered the GCE one.

00:40:04.324 --> 00:40:06.740
And then this is how these the
Kubernetes integration maps

00:40:06.740 --> 00:40:08.960
down to the key
Kubernetes resources.

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:12.081
So we'll come back to the
triggering in a second.

00:40:12.081 --> 00:40:14.330
But you're deploying Docker
images or container images

00:40:14.330 --> 00:40:15.240
in this case.

00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:17.680
You're not deploying
groups of VMs.

00:40:17.680 --> 00:40:19.750
You're just applying
replication controllers.

00:40:19.750 --> 00:40:22.180
And instead of containing
VMS, they contain pods.

00:40:22.180 --> 00:40:24.750
And pods can contain
multiple containers.

00:40:24.750 --> 00:40:27.369
We support all of
that throughout.

00:40:27.369 --> 00:40:28.160
And the same thing.

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:31.031
You can again apply those
same first class operations.

00:40:31.031 --> 00:40:32.530
So you can do a
red-black deployment

00:40:32.530 --> 00:40:35.474
or a blue-green deployment
on Kubernetes resources.

00:40:35.474 --> 00:40:37.140
And the triggering
is just what we said.

00:40:37.140 --> 00:40:39.745
Instead of looking for
necessarily a Jenkins job

00:40:39.745 --> 00:40:42.120
to complete, you could pull
on something like Docker hub.

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:45.450
And when you see a change
pushed, you kick off your job.

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:52.050
OK, so status.

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:56.180
This was open sourced
November 16, 2015.

00:40:56.180 --> 00:40:58.860
Quite comprehensive
support for Amazon's EC2

00:40:58.860 --> 00:41:00.624
and for Google Compute Engine.

00:41:00.624 --> 00:41:02.540
Pivotal has been adding
Cloud Foundry support.

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:04.150
It's quite far along.

00:41:04.150 --> 00:41:06.400
Microsoft Azure has as
a team working on it.

00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:07.520
We meet weekly.

00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:09.570
We're all working out
in the open together.

00:41:09.570 --> 00:41:12.549
It's been pretty
interesting trying

00:41:12.549 --> 00:41:13.590
to learn from each other.

00:41:13.590 --> 00:41:15.350
And we see kind of
the same struggles

00:41:15.350 --> 00:41:17.900
as we all come up to speed.

00:41:17.900 --> 00:41:20.279
But they're coming
along and they have also

00:41:20.279 --> 00:41:22.070
some sort of developer
conference coming up

00:41:22.070 --> 00:41:24.361
where they're going to be
presenting Spinnaker as well.

00:41:24.361 --> 00:41:28.184
So again, it supports a bunch
of different cloud platforms.

00:41:28.184 --> 00:41:30.350
So the Kubernetes support,
everything I showed today

00:41:30.350 --> 00:41:30.940
is checked in.

00:41:30.940 --> 00:41:32.550
I'm working basically from head.

00:41:32.550 --> 00:41:35.010
These are from the
published Debian packages.

00:41:35.010 --> 00:41:38.240
I just built an image
using the hosted scripts.

00:41:38.240 --> 00:41:40.250
This is being used in
production at Netflix.

00:41:40.250 --> 00:41:42.208
There are quite a few
other companies using it.

00:41:42.208 --> 00:41:45.420
We have some internal folks
at Google using it as well.

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:48.360
And we have a thriving
open source community.

00:41:48.360 --> 00:41:51.780
There's a Slack chat room
that there must be a thousand

00:41:51.780 --> 00:41:56.300
or close to a thousand users
now in a general channel there.

00:41:56.300 --> 00:41:58.850
We have some tags
on Stack Overflow.

00:41:58.850 --> 00:42:03.610
And obviously there are eight or
nine or 10 or more GitHub repos

00:42:03.610 --> 00:42:06.320
that host the various
services and all the umbrella

00:42:06.320 --> 00:42:08.581
projects that control it all.

00:42:08.581 --> 00:42:11.080
If you want to give it a shot,
on the Google Cloud Launcher,

00:42:11.080 --> 00:42:13.390
we have a quite easy
to use solution.

00:42:13.390 --> 00:42:15.310
It's a couple of clicks,
no configuration.

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:18.360
You'll be up and running
within your GCP project.

00:42:18.360 --> 00:42:21.180
And there are tutorials
on our Spinnaker.io page

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:23.590
and on our cloud
solutions page that

00:42:23.590 --> 00:42:25.780
will sort of point in
the right direction.

00:42:25.780 --> 00:42:28.640
We'll all be available
out in the playground.

00:42:28.640 --> 00:42:30.630
We're not sure where
that is, but they

00:42:30.630 --> 00:42:33.360
said to say that after this.

00:42:33.360 --> 00:42:35.960
And Andy brought some of
these guys from Netflix.

00:42:35.960 --> 00:42:38.470
So direct all the hard
questions to them.

00:42:38.470 --> 00:42:40.170
And thank you all for coming.

00:42:40.170 --> 00:42:41.520
Appreciate it.

00:42:41.520 --> 00:42:44.270
[APPLAUSE]

